<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Socialism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as socialist</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Socialism is a political and economic ideology advocating collective or state ownership and the redistribution of wealth. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> the Social Democratic Party grew into a mass movement. Accounts of the German Empire describe Bismarck's anti-socialist laws and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-insurance">social insurance</a> as responses to it.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The history of socialism has its origins in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Age of Enlightenment</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> of 1789, although it has precedents in earlier movements and ideas. Pierre Leroux claimed to have first used the word socialism, in the Parisian journal Le Globe in 1832. He was a follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-de-saint-simon">Henri de Saint-Simon</a>, one of the founders of what would later be labelled utopian socialism. The utopian socialists condemned liberal individualism for failing to address the poverty, oppression and wealth inequality of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>. Saint-Simon proposed economic planning and scientific administration, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-owen">Robert Owen</a> proposed cooperatives.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a> in 1847 and 1848, just before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848</a>, and called their position scientific socialism. In the last third of the 19th century parties dedicated to democratic socialism arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, drawing mainly on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>. For one week in December 1899 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australian-labor-party">Australian Labor Party</a> formed the first socialist government in the world, when it was elected in the Colony of Queensland under Premier Anderson Dawson.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Socialism encompasses diverse economic and social systems characterised by social ownership of the means of production, which can be public, community, collective, cooperative or employee ownership. Systems are divided into non-market forms and market socialism, which retains monetary prices and factor markets. In his Dictionary of Socialism (1924), Angelo S. Rappoport analysed forty definitions and found common elements: criticism of the social effects of private ownership of capital, a view that the solution is some form of collective control, and agreement that the result should be a society based on social justice. Major movements include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchism">anarchism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, the labour movement, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-democracy">social democracy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syndicalism">syndicalism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>By the early 1920s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-democracy">social democracy</a> had become the two dominant tendencies within the international socialist movement. In the first half of the 20th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the communist parties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Third International</a> came to represent socialism as a centrally planned economy directed by a state that owns all the means of production, although other trends condemned what they saw as its lack of democracy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, founded in 1949, introduced socialism through a state-led economy, land redistribution and the Anti-Rightist Movement, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a>.</p><p>Elsewhere reformist social democrats and democratic socialists came to power through the ballot box. In the UK, Herbert Morrison said that socialism is what the Labour government does, while Aneurin Bevan argued that it requires the main streams of economic activity to be brought under public direction. Such governments established mixed economies with partial nationalisation and social welfare, as in the Nordic model.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Socialism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Before the Second World War, Burma was the world's largest exporter of rice, but the Burmese Way to <b>Socialism</b> after 1962 nationalised trade and industry, and by 1987 the country was classed by the United Nations as one of the least developed in the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It took the view that &quot;<b>Socialism</b> cannot mean only liberation of the working people from the domination of exploiting class relations, but must make more provisions for a fuller life of the personality than any bourgeois democracy.&quot;</blockquote><a href="/movement/prague-spring">Prague Spring · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>According to William H. Friedland and Carl G. Rosberg Jr., the editors of the book African <b>Socialism</b>, its main architects were Julius Nyerere in Tanzania, Léopold Senghor in Senegal, Joseph Saidu Momoh and Siaka Stevens in Sierra Leone, Kwame Nkrumah and Hilla Limann in Ghana, François Tombalbaye in…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/african-socialism">African socialism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Ba'athism advocates a unified Arab state under a Ba'athist vanguard party and a revolutionary socialist framework, in the slogan &quot;Unity, Freedom, <b>Socialism</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/baathism">Ba'athism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>Anthony Crosland in The Future of <b>Socialism</b> (1956) and John Kenneth Galbraith in The Affluent Society (1958) described a future different from the one Marx had envisioned.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Joseph Schumpeter wrote in Capitalism, <b>Socialism</b> and Democracy (1942) that socialists would have to “administer” capitalism, and Irving Kristol called democratic socialism “an inherently unstable compound”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-democracy">Social democracy · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
